{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"4143","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-itsaplan-dispatch-runner.md@sha256:7800db89bfaf69c53c9c6df9e62a586b1f97a389582713bd28ad739932c3cf02"},"owner_reference":"4143","content":"# ARTIFACT:DISCOVERY\n\n## Feature\n\nOptional queued external-runner dispatch for development-harness milestones.\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nDevelopment-harness dispatch currently creates a local `spawn.py` child process and treats its local PID and result file as lifecycle evidence. Operators who already run an Itsaplan external agent runner cannot route eligible dispatch work through that queued runner without replacing or bypassing the local dispatch path. The requested outcome is to determine and later plan an optional dispatch mode that can queue work for an operator-controlled Itsaplan runner while preserving observable dispatch lifecycle guarantees.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. A later design can decide whether a queued-run route may coexist with current local spawning without altering the default behavior.\n2. Planning has source-backed requirements for queueing, claim/lease, run identity, result reporting, cancellation/recovery, authentication, permissions, working-directory/worktree boundaries, and evidence reconciliation.\n3. Any requirement that needs a running Itsaplan instance, credentials, runner process, or permission posture is explicit rather than assumed.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n\n1. Provisioning an Itsaplan instance, runner, credentials, MCP configuration, or external agent.\n2. Enabling unattended permission flags or broadening process permissions.\n3. Migrating the development-harness GitHub backlog backend to Itsaplan; that is the related but independent #4142 concern.\n4. Replacing local dispatch spawning before compatibility and operational evidence are established.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n\n1. If a queued dispatch route is adopted, an eligible dispatch item can be represented as a distinct Itsaplan queued run associated with a dispatch item and its title.\n2. The dispatch surface can distinguish queued, claimed, completed, failed, canceled, or recoverable external work from the local runner lifecycle without treating an unverified exit as successful completion.\n3. Completion evidence available to development-harness remains sufficient to report dispatch item status and a durable result.\n4. The route does not require configuring Itsaplan as development-harness backlog storage.\n\n### Non-Functional\n\n1. The existing local `dispatch spawn` behavior remains available unless an operator explicitly selects a future queued route.\n2. The route must not assume a server, agent key, runner process, coding-agent login, MCP enablement, or unattended permissions exist.\n3. Authentication and permission boundaries remain explicit and least-privilege; a queue handoff must not silently grant tool or filesystem access.\n4. Recovery must be observable after runner interruption, lease expiry, timeout, cancellation, or lost local worktree context.\n\n## Design Questions for Planning\n\n1. Evaluate whether direct REST enqueue, an API/MCP integration, or a dispatch adapter is the appropriate boundary for creating a queued run; do not treat the issue proposal as a committed mechanism.\n2. Evaluate how development-harness dispatch state can associate a milestone/wave/item with an Itsaplan run id and attempt identity without assuming those identities are interchangeable.\n3. Evaluate whether a runner working directory or a per-run worktree is needed to preserve current branch, worktree, and completion-evidence semantics.\n4. Evaluate cancellation and recovery semantics across development-harness state and Itsaplan leases before any lifecycle mapping is selected.\n\n## References\n\n- GitHub issue #4143.\n- `plugins/development-harness/dh_core/operations.py` local spawn and polling path.\n- `research/task-management/itsaplan.md`.\n- https://github.com/croffasia/itsaplan/blob/main/packages/runner/README.md\n- https://github.com/croffasia/itsaplan/blob/main/packages/runner/src/client.ts\n- https://github.com/croffasia/itsaplan/blob/main/apps/api/src/modules/agents/runner/index.ts\n\n## Resolved Questions\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Does the existing dispatch path create local processes? | Yes; `_build_spawn_cmd` creates a `uv run ... spawn.py` command and `_execute_spawn_item` reads its PID/result-file output. | `operations.py:1802-1824, 1923-1959` |\n| Does Itsaplan expose an external-runner queue protocol? | Yes; its runner claims `/agent-runs/claim`, heartbeats a run, and reports `/agent-runs/:runId/result` with success or failed. | upstream `packages/runner/src/client.ts`; `apps/api/src/modules/agents/runner/index.ts` |\n| Is #4142 required for this outcome? | No evidence establishes that dependency; #4142 addresses a WorkItemBackend provider, while #4143 concerns optional dispatch execution. | GitHub issues #4142 and #4143 |\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- Which actor/operator owns the instance, external-agent identity, API key, and permission posture for a future queued route?\n- Which existing dispatch lifecycle states and recovery guarantees are mandatory when external work is queued, and which may be surfaced as distinct states?\n- Can an external runner safely operate against the required repository/worktree/branch context, and who owns cleanup after timeout or cancellation?\n- Does the current upstream public enqueue/delegation surface provide all information needed to create a run for a development-harness dispatch item, or is an upstream integration boundary required?\n\n## User Confirmation\n\n- [ ] Grooming proceeded under the explicit request to research and preserve unresolved decisions; confirmation of a future execution design is not recorded."}